<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ashura">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashura</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashura is the tenth day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a>, the first month of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>. For Shia Muslims it is the day of mourning for the martyrdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a> in 680, marked by processions, sermons and passion plays. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims observe it as a day of voluntary fasting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashura.jpg" alt="Ashura" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Undetermined. Original uploader was Alborzagros · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%86%D9%82%D8%A7%D8%B4%D9%8A_%D8%B8%D9%87%D8%B1_%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%B4%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ashura is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> word meaning tenth, and shares its root with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> asor. According to the traditions, fasting on Ashura was a Jewish practice that Muhammad adopted after his arrival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 622, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> literature derives the observance from Yom Kippur. In a report in Sahih al-Bukhari, Muhammad finds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Medina fasting on the tenth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> and says that Muslims have more right to observe the fast. The fast ceased to be obligatory after about a year, when relations with the Jews of Medina deteriorated, a change often associated with verses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> that make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> the main month of fasting. Other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> traditions describe fasting on Ashura as a pre-Islamic practice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>For Shia Muslims, Ashura commemorates the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, grandson of Muhammad and third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, killed with most of his male relatives and his small retinue on 10 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> 61 AH (10 October 680) at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a> against the army of the Umayyad caliph Yazid ibn Mu'awiya. They had been surrounded for some days and deprived of the water of the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. The women and children were taken prisoner and marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. In Shia belief Karbala symbolises the struggle between good and evil, and mourning for Husayn is an act of protest against oppression and an act of worship.</p><p>Sunni Muslims also regard the killing of Husayn as a tragic event, but the ninth and tenth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> are days of voluntary fasting, strongly encouraged by jurists. Ashura is also associated with the parting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and Noah's disembarking from the Ark.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Shia Muslims commemorate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> through the months of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> and Safar, most of all in the first ten days of Muharram. Gatherings called majalis centre on the narration of the Karbala story and the recitation of elegies, intended to move the audience to tears. Processions march in major Shia cities, carrying objects such as the alam, the ensign of Husayn. Mild self-flagellation, striking the face and chest, is common, while extreme forms with knives or chains have been condemned by many clerics. Ta'ziyeh, a dramatic reenactment of the martyrdom, is performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and elsewhere.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> festivities on the day were established by the time of the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-taymiyya">Ibn Taymiyya</a>, who died in 1328, and may have grown up in response to Shia mourning: the Umayyad caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</a> made Ashura a festive public holiday to counter the commemoration of Husayn. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> the day is marked by fasting, almsgiving, special dishes, bonfires and carnivals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Buyids were Shia, and Mu'izz al-Dawla introduced public mourning for Husayn on <b>Ashura</b> and celebration of the feast of Ghadir in Baghdad in 963.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The killing of Ali’s son Husayn and his companions at Karbala in 680 by the forces of the Umayyad caliph Yazid became the founding tragedy of Shia Islam, mourned every year at <b>Ashura</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
